<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Sophene</h1><p class="st-meta">260 BCE – 95 BCE (approx.) · capital Arsamosata</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sophene, an Armenian kingdom in the upper Euphrates valley, ruled by a branch of the Orontid dynasty until Tigranes the Great absorbed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sophene lay west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> sources around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elazig">Elazığ</a>, the old land of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/isuwa">Isuwa</a>. In the 3rd century BCE an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontid-dynasty">Orontid</a> prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsames-i">Arsames</a>, ruled it and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsamosata">Arsamosata</a>. It was part of the wider Armenian world of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-orontid">Kingdom of Armenia (Orontid)</a>, though often under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> overlordship.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings controlled important crossings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zariadres">Zariadres</a> as governor; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defeated Antiochus in 189 BCE, Zariadres declared himself king. The court mixed Iranian and Greek traditions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 95 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad)</a> deposed its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artanes">Artanes</a>, and annexed Sophene, uniting <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sophene.jpg" alt="Sophene" width="320" height="188"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arshakuni_Armenia_150-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>260 BCE – 95 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arsamosata</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arsames, Zariadres, Artanes</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Sophene, Tsopk, Sophene</dd></dl></aside></div>
